  "worked_solution": "First, we need to find the equation of the parabola. From the diagram, we see that the parabola has a vertex at $(1,2)$ and passes through the point $(3,-2).$\n\nThe general standard equation of an up- or down-opening parabola with vertex $(h,k)$ is given by\n\n$$\n(x-h)^{2}=4p(y-k).\n$$\n\nSubstituting $(h,k)=(1,2)$ into the above gives\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}(x-1)^{2} & =4p(y-2).\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNow, we need to find $p.$ Since the parabola passes through the point $(3,-2),$ we substitute $x=3$ and $y=-2$ into the equation and get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}(3-1)^{2} & =4p(-2-2) \\\\ 4 & =4p(-4) \\\\ p & =-\\frac{1}{4}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, the equation of the parabola is\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}(x-1)^{2} & =-(y-2).\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nWhen the parabola intersects the $x$-axis, we must have $y=0.$ So, we substitute $y=0$ into the equation of the parabola and solve for $x:$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}(x-1)^{2} & =-(0-2) \\\\ (x-1)^{2} & =2 \\\\ x-1 & =\\pm \\sqrt{2} \\\\ x & =1\\pm \\sqrt{2}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, from left to right, the $x$-intercepts are $1-\\sqrt{2}$  and $1+\\sqrt{2}.$",
